# UST.G3FG: H02 Component-20 Canonical Conic-Graph Promotion No-Go

## Status

`CANONICAL_CONIC_GRAPH_RECONSTRUCTION_INCOMPLETE_MORE_EXACT_SOURCE_STRUCTURE_REQUIRED`

This theorem tests and rejects direct numerical promotion of the finite
component-20 kernels. It does not reject the conic filtration proved by
G3FF.

## Canonical Chart

G3FF proves at every one of 48 split fibers that

```text
K_15 direct_sum q^4 Sym^7 = Sym^15,
dim K_15 = 100,
dim q^4 Sym^7 = 36.
```

The integer multiplication matrix of `q^4=(xz-y^2)^4` has a fixed
36-by-36 pivot submatrix of determinant one. It therefore defines the same
chart in characteristic zero and at every prime. Projection along this
fourth-normal carrier canonically normalizes each finite kernel as a graph

```text
K_15 = graph(T_gamma),  T_gamma: k^100 -> k^36.
```

This removes every arbitrary finite-kernel basis choice.

## Reconstruction Test

For each of eight split primes, the six graph values are interpolated in the
power basis `1,gamma,...,gamma^5`. This gives

```text
6 * 36 * 100 = 21,600
```

coefficient slots per characteristic. The first seven primes give a
109-bit CRT modulus and rational-reconstruction uniqueness bound

```text
13,400,828,956,057,273.
```

Of the 21,600 slots:

```text
13,162 admit a unique seven-prime rational candidate;
 8,438 lie outside that uniqueness range;
     0 candidates pass the independent eighth prime.
```

Thus the 100-dimensional finite graph is not the reduction of one fixed
bounded-height `Q(gamma)` graph in this canonical chart.

## Positive Control

The identical evaluation, six-root interpolation, seven-prime CRT and
eighth-prime holdout pipeline is applied to the exact G3FF `D7` source.
All 216 power-basis slots reconstruct literally, and all 216 pass the
holdout with zero aliases. The recovered integer coordinates agree exactly
with the stored `D7`, including maximum absolute value 590.

The graph rejection is therefore not caused by root ordering, interpolation,
CRT, rational reconstruction or the holdout implementation.

## Consequence

Adding more arbitrary split primes is not the proof route. The stable finite
invariant is the conic normal filtration, while the individual 100-plane
varies between reductions. Characteristic-zero promotion must occur before
kernel specialization, through the selected source operator and its
normal-jet homotopy.

This is precisely the distinction later used by G3FH: finite kernel graphs
do not promote directly, but the exact source descriptors select the same
`100+36` conic decomposition before projection.

## Claim Boundary

G3FG does not prove that the characteristic-zero kernel has dimension below
100, and it does not disprove an upstream exact 100-dimensional kernel
constructed in a different presentation. It proves only that the canonical
finite graphs themselves do not form one promoted `Q(gamma)` graph under the
certified height and independent-holdout test.

